{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":1028681,"num_turns":65,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"9d8dac65-33e2-4345-bcc0-be12ca2f4c63","total_cost_usd":5.058614999999999,"usage":{"input_tokens":654865,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":71258,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":655308,"outputTokens":71283,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":5.058614999999999,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"All 16 workloads pass, ~2.8x faster than the reference. Done — `solution.json` and `kernel.py` are in place.\n\n## What this problem actually is\n\nGenerating 64 floats (256 B) from a scalar. The arithmetic is free and the memory traffic is one cache line, so there is no throughput story here — the Speed-of-Light bound is essentially *one kernel launch*. The reference spends 21 µs running four elementwise kernels (`arange → div → pow → reciprocal`) plus an allocation. Everything worth winning is launch overhead.\n\n## The part that nearly went wrong\n\nThe tolerance looked generous until I did the arithmetic: `required_matched_ratio` is 0.99 over 64 elements, and 63/64 = 0.984. **Every single element must land within ~1 ulp** — one sloppy lane fails the workload. That killed the two natural implementations:\n\n- Triton's fp32 `libdevice.pow` — up to **20 ulps** off across these thetas.\n- `exp2(-e * log2f(theta))` in pure fp32 — rounding `log2(theta)` (~23.25 at θ=1e7) to fp32 is already ~1e-6 relative error.\n\nBoth would have looked plausible and failed. The fix is a Dekker split of `log2(theta)` host-side into an exact fp32 `hi` plus fp64 remainder `lo`, reconstructed and evaluated in fp64 on-device. The exponent `i·(2/128) = i/64` is dyadic, so it contributes no rounding of its own. Result: ≤2 ulp everywhere, worst case using **0.75 of the tolerance budget** — passing with margin, not scraping by.\n\nI checked all 16 thetas locally against the reference before spending any verify attempt, which is why attempt 1 passed clean and 2 remain unused.\n\n## Where the speed came from\n\nMeasured decomposition rather than guessed: device time is ~2.25 µs (CUDA-graph replay), but Triton's `JITFunction.__call__` adds ~7.6 µs of pure CPU dispatch on top — it re-derives the signature and hashes the specialization on every call. Compiling once at import and invoking the bound `CompiledKernel.run` cuts that to ~3.4 µs.\n\nI confirmed that's the actual floor rather than assuming: a raw `ctypes` `hipModuleLaunchKernel` came in at 3.97 µs vs Triton's 4.03 µs — no gain, Triton's C launcher is already at the HIP driver limit. I stopped optimizing there.\n\nTwo judgment calls worth flagging:\n\n- **Stream handle is re-read every call**, not cached. `torch.cuda.current_stream()` costs 2.4 µs, but `torch._C._cuda_getCurrentRawStream` costs 0.06 µs — so correctness under side streams and graph capture is essentially free. Verified both.\n- **I rejected a ring-buffer allocation trick.** Slicing a preallocated buffer saves ~0.3 µs but aliases outputs across calls, which is the \"caching outputs\" failure mode. Not worth it for 0.3 µs, and wrong regardless.\n\nThe `CompiledKernel.run` positional layout is a Triton internal that has shifted between releases, so it's probed at import against the real launcher and numerically validated, with a fallback to the ordinary JIT path. One bug I caught here: my first probe validated with `torch.equal`, but the fp32 `pow` baseline differs by 1–2 ulp — it silently rejected a *valid* layout and fell back to the slow path (10.9 µs). Local benchmarking caught the regression; a tolerance-based check fixed it.\n\nVerified from a clean tree in a fresh process, matching how the final scoring run loads it. Scratch files removed.","ttft_ms":3739,"ttft_stream_ms":3737,"time_to_request_ms":89,"type":"result","duration_ms":1122710,"uuid":"82442b12-59c4-4ecb-b786-01777f57d4a7"}
